<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juliusspital</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Juliusspital is a charitable foundation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded in 1576 by Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-echter-von-mespelbrunn">Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn</a> as a hospital and home for the poor, sick and elderly. Endowed with large estates and vineyards, it still runs a hospital, a care home and one of the largest wine estates in Germany, and its baroque buildings stand in the city centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juliusspital.jpg" alt="Juliusspital" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 12345678 · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juliusspital-Wuerzburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Julius Echter, Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, founded the hospital in 1576 on the ground of a Jewish cemetery, endowing it with the property of the abandoned Monastery of Sancta Vallis in Heiligenthal. In its early years it took in pilgrims, people suffering from epilepsy and orphans as well as the sick and poor. Today the foundation, Stiftung Juliusspital Würzburg, includes both the hospital and the Juliusspital winery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The foundation also held rights of lordship in the countryside. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> ended in 1648, part of the village of Thüngen came to the Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and was administered by the Juliusspital, and in Gräfendorf the Barons of Thüngen and the Juliusspital shared the lordship. With the German Mediatisation of 1803 these rights passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and in 1805 to the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a>; those in Karsbach went to the newly formed Grand Duchy of Würzburg under the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>.</p><p>The hospital became a centre of medical teaching. Carl Caspar von Siebold, head physician from 1776, introduced new surgical techniques and a regimen of hygiene, and in 1805 the Juliusspital reportedly had the first modern operating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in the world. Cajetan von Textor became professor of surgery there in 1816; among his students was Bernhard Heine, inventor of the osteotome. Franz von Rinecker directed psychiatry from 1863 and dermatology from 1872. In 1878 The Examiner reported that the large and rich hospital made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>'s medical faculty the most numerous of its university.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The baroque complex is built round a courtyard with a church. Its northern wing, 160 m long, was added by Antonio Petrini in 1700–4, with a wine cellar of similar size beneath it, and the rococo pharmacy survived the bombing of 1945 largely undamaged. North of the hospital lies a park for the patients, with the Alte Anatomie built by Joseph Greising in 1705–14, where Albert von Kölliker and Rudolf Virchow taught up to 48 students, and a water basin by Jacob van der Auvera with figures of the Franconian rivers Main, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauber">Tauber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> and Sinn.</p><p>The foundation still runs a hospital and a retirement home. Its wine estate, of 177 hectares in the Franconian wine region, is the second largest winery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and a member of the Association of German Prädikat Wine Estates.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stiftung Juliusspital Würzburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn refounded the university in 1582, built the <b>Juliusspital</b> and drove a forceful Counter-Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-wurzburg">Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg</a></li></ul></section>
